globalThis.COURSE_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"pageId":"assignments/hw01","pageTitle":"Homework 1","heading":"Digits on a pentagon, without repetition","anchor":"digits-on-a-pentagon-without-repetition","text":"Digits on a pentagon, without repetition Points: 12 Place five distinct digits chosen from at the vertices of a regular pentagon. Two patterns are considered the same when one can be obtained from the other by rotating the pentagon. Reflections are not considered the same. The examples below illustrate this convention. The labels name the fixed vertex positions , beginning at the top vertex and proceeding clockwise. Pentagon with digits 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 clockwise from the top vertex. A rotation of Pattern 1. A nonrotation of Pattern 1 using digits 1, 2, 3, 5, 4. A pattern using digits 1, 2, 4, 6, 8. Patterns 1 and 2 are considered the same because a rotation carries one to the other. No other pair among the four displayed patterns is considered the same. How many different patterns are there? Need a free hint? First treat as fixed, distinguishable positions and count the assignments of five distinct digits. Next determine how many labeled arrangements represent each pattern up to rotation, explaining why the ``no repetition’’ condition makes this number the same for every pattern. Finally apply the division rule and simplify to a numerical answer. Pentagon with digits 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 clockwise from the top vertex. A rotation of Pattern 1, with digits 5, 1, 2, 3, 4 clockwise from the top vertex. Pentagon with digits 1, 2, 3, 5, 4 clockwise from the top vertex. Pentagon with digits 1, 2, 4, 6, 8 clockwise from the top vertex."},{"pageId":"assignments/hw01","pageTitle":"Homework 1","heading":"Forming a committee","anchor":"forming-a-committee","text":"Forming a committee Points: 10 A company has 20 employees in Marketing and 10 employees in Research and Development (R&D). It must form a committee with the following five positions: one chair; one vice-chair; and three ordinary members, whose positions are not distinguished from one another. No employee may hold more than one position. The chair and vice-chair must come from different departments; the ordinary members may come from either department. How many committees are possible? Need a free hint? Split into cases according to which department provides the chair and which provides the vice-chair. Choose the officers first, then the three ordinary members; remember that the ordinary-member positions are not distinguished."},{"pageId":"assignments/hw01","pageTitle":"Homework 1","heading":"Homework 1","anchor":"homework-1","text":"Homework 1 Due: Thursday, August 27, 2026 23:58Submit: Gradescope, through Blackboard’s assignment area. Please write your homework by hand. Start each exercise on a new page, scan your work to a legible PDF, and clearly mark the pages associated with every exercise."},{"pageId":"assignments/hw01","pageTitle":"Homework 1","heading":"Using every letter","anchor":"using-every-letter","text":"Using every letter Points: 10 How many strings of length 10 over the alphabet contain each of the three letters at least once? Need a free hint? Count all strings, then subtract the forbidden strings. A forbidden string uses exactly one or exactly two different letters. How many use only , only , or only ? For each of the pairs , , and , how many strings use both letters in the pair and no third letter?"},{"pageId":"assignments/hw01","pageTitle":"Homework 1","heading":"Why division by five can fail","anchor":"why-division-by-five-can-fail","text":"Why division by five can fail Points: 8 Now change Exercise 3 by allowing digits to repeat. Thus each of the five vertices may receive any digit from , while patterns that differ only by a rotation are still considered the same. A tempting argument is: There are assignments to five fixed vertices, and every pattern is represented five times by rotation, so the answer is . Explain carefully why this argument is incorrect. You are not being asked to find the correct total. Need a free hint? Explain why is the correct number of assignments when the vertices are fixed and labeled. Then list the distinct rotations represented by each of the clockwise strings 00000, 00001, and 01234, beginning at vertex , and use them to identify which requirement of the division rule fails. Focus on whether every pattern is counted the same number of times."},{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Academic integrity, collaboration, and artificial intelligence","anchor":"academic-integrity-collaboration-and-artificial-intelligence","text":"Academic integrity, collaboration, and artificial intelligence You are encouraged to work with classmates, tutors, and me: talking through ideas, definitions, and problem-solving strategies is a valuable part of learning. You must understand every solution you submit and write it based on your own understanding. Please do not copy another person’s solution or write-up. You may use generative artificial intelligence as a study aid, but not to produce any work that you submit for credit. It is fine to ask a study question such as, ``I do not understand when to use generating functions to count combinations with restrictions.’’ It is not acceptable to ask an AI system how to solve a specific assigned problem. Rephrasing a generated solution using your own words is not acceptable. If you are unsure whether a particular use is allowed, ask me before you submit your work. When you submit an assignment, disclose any collaboration or use of external tools, even when it is permitted."},{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Assignments","anchor":"assignments","text":"Assignments"},{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Online questions","anchor":"online-questions","text":"Online questions Online questions usually follow each lecture and are due at the start of our next class meeting. They are available through Blackboard’s online-question area; deadlines appear in the Blackboard Calendar. Online questions provide a quick chance to practice vocabulary and core skills before we build on the material. Most questions allow multiple attempts."},{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Written homework","anchor":"written-homework","text":"Written homework Assignment Due Homework 1 2026-08-27 23:59 Submit your work through Gradescope using Blackboard’s assignment area. The printable version is available from each assignment page. Weekly homework gives you a chance to work through more involved problems using the techniques we have learned. A complete solution includes both a clear explanation and correct computations. Every exercise is required; point values indicate how much each exercise contributes to the assignment grade. Please write your homework by hand. For each exercise, start a new page. Scan your homework to PDF for submission. Submit it on Gradescope and clearly mark the pages associated with every exercise. Solutions will be posted here within one week of the submission deadline. The deadlines will also appear in the Blackboard Calendar."},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Calendar","anchor":"calendar","text":"Calendar I will put all deadlines on the Blackboard Calendar. Please check it regularly."},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Daily lecture log","anchor":"calendar:daily-log","text":"Daily lecture log The public daily log below contains the lectures currently released. Later planning entries remain in the instructor’s private lecture log and do not appear in this navigation. Wk Lec Topics Ref 1 L1: Set notation and basic counting 2026-08-17 Sets, cardinality, Cartesian products, and the sum rule. Set notation and basic counting. L2: Product rule and strings 2026-08-19 Product rule; strings with and without repetition. Set notation and basic counting. L3: More counting techniques 2026-08-21 Subtraction, division, permutations, combinations, and counting models. More counting techniques. Tentative Lecture topics and assessment dates may change."},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Exams","anchor":"exams","text":"Exams Date Assessment Friday, September 18 Tentative Midterm 1 Friday, October 16 Tentative Midterm 2 Friday, November 13 Tentative Midterm 3 8:00-10:00 a.m., Wednesday, December 9, 2026 Exam 4 and mastery reassessment"},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Key university dates","anchor":"key-university-dates","text":"Key university dates For official University dates, see the Fall 2026 academic calendar and the Registrar’s final-exam schedule. Date Event Monday, August 17 Classes begin Monday, September 7 Labor Day holiday Tuesday, November 24–Friday, November 27 Thanksgiving holiday Wednesday, December 9 Final exam: 8:00-10:00 a.m., Wednesday, December 9, 2026"},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Week and unit overview","anchor":"calendar:week-summary","text":"Week and unit overview Weeks Unit Topic 1–4 Tentative Unit I: Enumeration Counting models, bijections, double counting, and inclusion-exclusion 4–5 Tentative Unit II: Generating-function foundations Formal power series and ordinary generating-function operations 6–8 Tentative Unit III: Recurrences State models, linear recurrences, recursive evaluation, and dynamic programming 9–16 Tentative Further topics To be announced"},{"pageId":"index","pageTitle":"Combinatorics","heading":"Combinatorics","anchor":"combinatorics","text":"Combinatorics Course: MATH 31003-01Semester: Fall 2026Instructor: Gennady UraltsevMeetings: Monday, Wednesday, Friday 8:35-9:25 a.m., GEAR 108 Welcome to Combinatorics! We will explore the mathematics of discrete objects: the many ways things can be selected, arranged, connected, and counted. The course covers basic combinatorial techniques, the principle of inclusion-exclusion, generating functions, and recursive relations. Additional topics may include modular arithmetic and applications to encryption, coding theory, graph theory, counting with symmetries, and algebraic structures."},{"pageId":"index","pageTitle":"Combinatorics","heading":"Start here","anchor":"start-here","text":"Start here Syllabus: how the course works, grading, materials, and what to expect. Course calendar: class dates, current topics, holidays, and exams. Assignments: written homework and online questions. Resources: references and student-support links. Lecture notes: notes organized by course unit and lecture. Please check the course calendar for holidays, University deadlines, and topic updates. Course announcements, grades, and submission links are maintained in Blackboard."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/index","pageTitle":"Lecture Notes","heading":"Course units","anchor":"course-units","text":"Course units Unit I: enumeration and inclusion-exclusion. Unit II: generating-function foundations. Unit III: recurrence models. Later units: further topics as announced on the calendar."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/index","pageTitle":"Lecture Notes","heading":"Lecture Notes","anchor":"lecture-notes","text":"Lecture Notes These notes are the main guide through the course. They are organized by unit and lecture; draft and future units remain in the instructor’s private course design collection until they are ready for publication."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1. Set notation and basic counting","anchor":"set-notation-and-basic-counting","text":"1. Set notation and basic counting Combinatorics is all about counting objects. We introduce the notation for “containers” of objects a.k.a. sets and define operations on sets. Next, we study the first two counting methods: the sum rule and product rule."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.1 Set notation","anchor":"set-notation","text":"1.1 Set notation A set is a collection of distinct objects. We usually denote a set by a capital letter, such as , and an element of a set by a lowercase letter, such as . The notation means that is an element of , while means that it is not. We can describe a set by listing its elements surrounded by braces. Example 1.1.1 (The decimal-digit set) is the set of decimal digits. Because a set records membership rather than order, . Repeating an element also does not change a set: . We can also describe a set by a property its elements satisfy. If is a specified universe of possible elements, then we use the notation The colon is read as “such that”. Example 1.1.2 (Sets described by a property) is the set of even integers, where denotes the integers. If , then The symbol denotes the empty set, the set with no elements."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.1.1 Set operations","anchor":"set-operations","text":"1.1.1 Set operations Let and be subsets of the same universe. The intersection of and consists of the elements belonging to both sets: The union of and consists of the elements belonging to at least one of the sets: Here “or” is inclusive: an element belonging to both and belongs to their union. The set difference, also called the relative complement, consists of the elements of that do not belong to : The Cartesian product of two sets and is the set of ordered pairs with chosen from and chosen from : For more than two sets we can write . If all the sets are the same we write and so on. Two sets are disjoint if they have no elements in common; equivalently, . Example 1.1.3 (Operations on two finite sets) If and , then"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.1.2 Cardinality and inclusion","anchor":"cardinality-and-inclusion","text":"1.1.2 Cardinality and inclusion For a finite set , its cardinality is the number of elements in . Example 1.1.4 (Cardinalities of finite sets) and . We write if every element of is also an element of : We write if but . Two sets are equal precisely when they have the same elements: Thus, to prove , it is enough to prove both and ."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.1.3 Logical quantifiers and summation notation","anchor":"logical-quantifiers-and-summation-notation","text":"1.1.3 Logical quantifiers and summation notation The symbol means “for every”. The symbol means “there exists”. Example 1.1.5 (Reading quantifiers) means that every element of is nonnegative. says that at least one element of has square . The symbol means “there exists a unique.” Thus asserts two things: at least one element of satisfies , and no two different elements of both satisfy . The notation means that we add the terms as the integer index runs from through : Example 1.1.6 (The sum-of-squares formula in summation notation) An important formula we will prove later in the course is"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.2 Introduction to counting","anchor":"introduction-to-counting","text":"1.2 Introduction to counting"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.2.1 What is a counting problem?","anchor":"counting-problem-model","text":"1.2.1 What is a counting problem? A counting problem asks for the cardinality of a finite set. The first task is therefore to identify that set. We need to know: what one outcome is; when two outcomes are considered different; what restrictions an outcome must satisfy. Example 1.2.1 (Distinguished dice summing to ten) Consider two ordinary six-sided dice, one red and one blue. How many ways can one obtain a roll whose sum is Because the dice are distinguished by color, an outcome is an ordered pair , where is the red result and is the blue result. We can list all of them The outcomes whose sum is are , so there are three. Now let us change the problem in two different ways: Example 1.2.2 (What counts as a reroll outcome?) Variant 1 Consider two ordinary six-sided dice, one red and one blue. If a roll results in both dice having the same number, we reject the roll and try again. How many ways can one obtain a roll whose sum is . Variant 2 Consider two ordinary six-sided dice, one red and one blue. If a roll results in both dice having the same number, we reroll the red die until the two numbers are not the same. How many ways can one obtain a roll whose sum is . It is not fully clear what the problem is asking us. If a “way” records the entire history of rolls, then, in variant 1, rolling and then rerolling and obtaining is different from rolling . Actually, arbitrarily many ties can occur before an accepted result, so there are infinitely many possible histories. Moreover, in variant 2, if one first rolls there is actually no way to ever get as a sum. However, if we count only the final accepted ordered pair, then the problem becomes simple: is not allowed, and the two outcomes with sum are and just as before. This is true whether we reroll both dice (variant 1) after a tie or keep the red die and reroll only the blue die (variant 2). This example shows that it is important to clearly understand what the problem, written in English, means at a precise mathematical level. % horizontal rule in the original Markdown Next, we will develop tools that will help us solve the following challenge problem. You can try to solve it on your own, or come back to it once we covered the necessary counting techniques. For now, you should read and understand it, and use it as a motivating example for the things we will learn next Problem 1.2.3 (Committees with and without a chair) A company has 20 employees and they need to form a committee of 4 people. How many ways can this be done? A company has 20 employees and they need to form a committee of 4 people: 1 chair and 3 other general committee members. How many ways can this be done?"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.2.2 The sum rule","anchor":"the-sum-rule","text":"1.2.2 The sum rule Suppose one object is chosen from either of two finite sets and . If the sets are disjoint, then every object in belongs to exactly one of the two sets. We may therefore add their cardinalities. Theorem 1.2.4 (Sum rule) If and are finite disjoint sets, then Example 1.2.5 (Lowercase letters or decimal digits) A single character may be either a lowercase English letter or a decimal digit. How many allowed characters are there? Solution: Let , and . There are lowercase letters and digits. No character is both a lowercase letter and a digit, so . The sum rule gives If uppercase English letters are also allowed, let be their set. The sets , , and are pairwise disjoint, and the number of allowed characters is The same reasoning extends to any finite number of cases. Theorem 1.2.6 (General sum rule for disjoint sets) Let be finite pairwise disjoint sets. Pairwise disjoint means that Then We can rephrase the sum rule in terms of outcomes of procedures, rather tnan sets. Theorem 1.2.7 (General sum rule for mutually exclusive cases) Suppose the outcomes of a task are divided into mutually exclusive cases , and every outcome belongs to exactly one case. The total number of outcomes is the sum of the numbers in the cases. Problem (Check yourself) In the character example, identify , , and in the general sum rule, and verify pairwise disjointness. Disjointness is important! In the next example, let us see why checking disjointness is very important. Example 1.2.8 (Overlapping brands at two stores) Suppose Amazon sells three brands of juice and Walmart sells five brands. How many different brands can a shopper choose from if they may shop at either store? Wrong solution: The number of brands at amazon is and the number of brands at Walmart is , we need to find the total number of brands . The sum rule tells us that The problem with this solution is that if the stores carry some of the same brands, adding gives duplicates. is the correct answer only if and do not carry brands in common, that is if . Depending on the overlap, the available information permits any total from through . Warning 1.2.9 (Check the sum-rule hypotheses) Before using the sum rule, check that the cases are disjoint and that together they include every outcome being counted."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.2.3 The product rule","anchor":"the-product-rule","text":"1.2.3 The product rule Suppose an outcome is constructed through a fixed sequence of choices. If every complete sequence of choices produces exactly one outcome different choices produce different outcomes, the number of options at each step of the sequence of choices is fixed and does not depend on the choices made in the other steps, then the total number of outcomes is obtained by multiplying the numbers of options at each step. Example 1.2.10 (Two-character lowercase strings) A two-character string is an ordered sequence in which each entry is one of the 26 lowercase letters a-z. Solution: There are choices for and, after any first choice, choices for . Therefore there are such strings. Note: The strings and are different because their entries occur in different positions. The argument allows multiple steps and also allows the specific choices at each step to depend on the other choices made. It is important that the total number of choices at each step remains the same. Theorem 1.2.11 (Product rule) Suppose every outcome is constructed uniquely by a fixed sequence of steps. Assume that, after any valid choices in the earlier steps, step has exactly valid options. Then the number of outcomes is The available options may depend on the earlier choices; what must remain fixed is their number . Example 1.2.12 (Three-letter strings with repetition) How many strings of length 3 made with letters a-z are there? Solution: A string can be specified by first choosing the letter in position 1 (step 1), then the letter in position 2 (step 2), then in position 3 (step 3). For each of the steps there are 26 choices. Every string is obtained from exactly one sequence of such choices (different choices give different strings), so the product rule gives Example 1.2.13 (Three-letter strings without repetition) How many strings of length 3 made with letters a-z are there such that no letter appears more than once? Solution: A string can be specified by first choosing the letter in position 1 (step 1), then the letter in position 2 (step 2) and making sure that it is not the same as the letter chosen in position 1, then choosing a letter in position 3 (step 3) while making sure that it is not the letter chosen in position 1 nor in position 2. Step 1: 26 choices Step 2: 25 choices (26 letters except the one chosen in step 1) Step 3: 24 choices (26 letters except the one chosen in step 1 and the one chosen in step 2) Every valid string is obtained from exactly one sequence of such choices (different choices give different strings), so the product rule gives It is important to note that in this example the specific choices at steps 2 and 3 depend on the choices made in the previous step but the number of possible choices does not! This allows us to apply the product rule. Example 1.2.14 (Three-letter strings with no symbol appearing three times) How many strings of length 3 made with letters a-z are there such that no letter appears more than twice? Wrong/Incomplete solution: A string can be specified by first choosing the letter in position 1 (step 1), then the letter in position 2 (step 2) (without any restrictions, because the second letter can be the same as the first one and not lead to a forbidden outcome) In step 3 we choose the letter for position 3. Step 1: 26 choices Step 2: 26 choices Step 3: ?? choices (it depends, if in step 1 and 2 we chose the same letter then we cannot choose it again so we have 25 choices; if we did not choose the same letter we can choose any letter, so 26 choices. ) Possible correct solution: A string can be specified by choosing by first choosing the letter in position 1 (step 1), then the letter in position 2 (step 2), then the letter in position 3 (step 3), all while making sure not to choose a string with the same letter repeated 3 times. Let us distinguish 2 cases: D: In step 1 and step 2 we choose different letters S: In step 1 and step 2 we choose the same letter By the sum rule, the total amount of strings is going to be since (we cannot both select the same and different letters in step 1 and 2). Let us compute . Since the first two letters are different we have the following options: Step 1: 26 choices Step 2: 25 choices (cannot be the same as step 1) Step 3: 26 choices (since the first are different, there are no restrictions) so by the product rule Let us compute . Since the first two letters are the same we have the following options: Step 1: 26 choices Step 2: 1 choice (must be the same letter as step 1) Step 3: 25 choices (since the first two letters are the same we cannot choose that letter again) so The final answer is ."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.3 Strings: an important type of counting problems","anchor":"strings-model","text":"1.3 Strings: an important type of counting problems The preceding examples illustrate an important type of counting problem: counting ordered sequences of symbols. Definition 1.3.1 (String over an alphabet) Let be a finite set of symbols that we call an alphabet. A string of length over an alphabet is an ordered sequence where each . Using set notation, a string of length is an element of the Cartesian product . The position of each symbol is important. For example, ab and ba are different strings. Two strings are equal precisely when they have the same symbol in every position. Repetition is allowed unless the problem states otherwise. There is also one string of length , called the empty string. It contains no symbols. Theorem 1.3.2 (Strings with repetition) Let be an alphabet with symbols, and let be a nonnegative integer. The number of strings of length over is Proof There are positions. Each position may contain any of the symbols, independently of the symbols chosen in the other positions. The product rule therefore gives When , the product has no factors and is equal to , corresponding to the one empty string. Example 1.3.3 (Three-digit strings with repetition) Let be the alphabet of decimal digits. The number of strings of length over is These are strings rather than three-digit positive integers, so a string such as 042 is included. Suppose now that no symbol may occur more than once. Theorem 1.3.4 (Strings without repetition) Let be an alphabet with symbols, and let be a nonnegative integer. The number of strings of length over with no repeated symbol is This formula makes sense when , the product in the first line has factors. When we have . When then . Proof Assuming that . There are choices for the first position. Once a symbol has been used, it cannot be used again, so there are choices for the second position, choices for the third position, and, finally, choices for the th position. The product rule gives The specific digits available at each step depend on the earlier choices, but the number of available digits does not. This is exactly what the product rule requires. If , a string without repetition would require more than distinct symbols from an alphabet containing only symbols. No such string exists, so . Example 1.3.5 (Three-digit strings without repetition) Let be the alphabet of decimal digits. The number of strings of length with no repeating digits over is These are strings rather than three-digit positive integers, so a string such as 042 is included."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.4 External References","anchor":"external-references","text":"1.4 External References"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.4.1 Additional material (optional)","anchor":"additional-material-optional","text":"1.4.1 Additional material (optional) Counting Rocks! - Basics of Set Theory Discrete Math II - 6.1.1 The Rules of Sum and Product Counting Rocks! - The addition, subtraction, and multiplication principles"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2. More counting techniques","anchor":"more-counting-techniques","text":"2. More counting techniques We will now add two operations to our counting toolkit. The difference rule counts allowed objects by removing forbidden ones. The division principle corrects a construction that overcounts the same outcome multiple times. We then use these rules to derive formulas for permutations and combinations - important counting problems that differ by whether order is considered important or not. Finally, we discuss how strings and sets can be used to model a variety of different combinatorial problems using bijections."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.1 The difference rule","anchor":"the-difference-rule","text":"2.1 The difference rule We used Theorem 1.2.4 to count the elements in a set by subdividing them into separate cases. When solving problems that impose restrictions on the allowed outcomes, it is convenient to first count the total number of possible outcomes and subtract those that are not allowed. Theorem 2.1.1 (Difference rule) Let be a finite set. If then Equivalently, if is the universe of all possible objects and is the set of forbidden objects, then the number of allowed objects is . Proof Because , the sets and are disjoint and their union is . The sum rule therefore gives Subtracting from both sides proves the formula. Example 2.1.2 (Recounting strings by subtraction) In Example 1.2.14, we counted the length- strings over a-z in which no letter appears three times by separating them into cases. Let us now count the same set using subtraction. Solution: An outcome is an ordered string of three letters. Let be the set of all such strings. Repetition is allowed, so the product rule gives Let be the set of forbidden strings in which one letter appears three times. Such a string is determined by its repeated letter, so . The difference rule gives This is a useful way of counting because forbidden strings are easier to describe than the allowed strings. This agrees with the earlier case-based count, but subtraction produces the shorter model. Warning 2.1.3 (Subtract only objects inside the universe) Suppose and . A careless application of the difference rule gives whereas, actually, The reason for the mistake is that we didn’t check that because . The elements of that can actually be removed are those in . Therefore the correct computation is Before subtracting, always check that the forbidden set is a subset of the universe! If it is not, replace it by its intersection with the universe. Example 2.1.4 (Distinct-digit PINs) A four-digit PIN is a string of four digits from 0-9; leading zeroes are allowed. How many four-digit PINs have no repeated digit and contain at least one odd digit? Incorrect solution: Let be the set of four-digit PINs with no repeated digit. Because a PIN is an ordered string of length 4 chosen from of an alphabet of 10 letters, we get We want to remove the PINs containing no odd digit. The number of strings containing no odd digits is the strings of 4 digits chosen from the alphabet of the 5 odd digits : Therefore we get that This solution is wrong! The issue is that cannot be subtracted from because it is not true that . The way we counted is we included pins containing no odd digits but possibly containing repetitions, like the pin 2248. This pin was not accounted for in so we should not be subtracting it. Correct solution: Instead, let be the set of PINs in whose digits are all even. Then , and its four positions must contain distinct digits chosen from the 5 even digits. Hence The difference rule now gives The modeling lesson is that the universe and the forbidden set must enforce the same background restrictions. Here, “no repeated digit” is part of the definition of both and ."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.1.1 Counting the same set in two ways","anchor":"counting-the-same-set-in-two-ways","text":"2.1.1 Counting the same set in two ways In Example 1.2.14 and Example 2.1.2, we counted the same quantity in two different ways. Our approaches clearly work for any alphabet with letters (it doesn’t have to be the standard 26 letter alphabet) The first approach applied to an alphabet of letters, where gives the formula On the other hand, using the difference rule gives the formula Because both expressions count the same set of strings, we must surely have By itself, this identity is not very exciting. One can show it by standard algebraic simplification. However, it is interesting because it shows how using different combinatorial approaches can provide a “combinatorial” explanation to important formulas from algebra. This idea is often referred to as double counting, and we will see more examples in the future."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.1.2 More examples of subtraction","anchor":"more-examples-of-subtraction","text":"2.1.2 More examples of subtraction Example 2.1.5 (Decimal strings containing a zero) How many length- strings of decimal digits contain at least one 0? Solution: The objects are strings, so order matters, leading zeroes are allowed, and digits may repeat. Let be the set of all length- decimal strings. Then . We could try to count strings according to the position of a zero. However the cases would overlap because there are string that have several zeroes. Instead we exclude the strings with no zero. Each of the six positions has choices, so there are of them. Hence the requested count is The complement replaces overlapping cases with one product-rule count. Example 2.1.6 (A string with prohibition) How many length- strings over the alphabet do not begin with ab? Solution: Let contain all length- strings over the alphabet. Repetition is allowed, so . Let contain the strings beginning with ab. Their first two positions are fixed, and each of the remaining six positions has choices. Thus , and the difference rule gives The restriction fixes two particular positions; it does not forbid a or b in the other positions. Example 2.1.7 (Strings containing at least two different symbols) How many length- strings over an alphabet of letters with , contain at least two different letters? Solution: There are strings in total. If a string does NOT have two different letters then it is just one letter repeated 5 times like aaaaa. So there are forbidden strings. Therefore the quantity we are looking for is Note: for the boundary case (that is an alphabet of one letter), this gives , as it should! There is no way to have at least two different letters if all you have is one letter."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.2 The division principle and permutations","anchor":"the-division-principle-and-permutations","text":"2.2 The division principle and permutations Theorem 1.2.11 requires every outcome to be constructed uniquely by the specified sequence of choices. The division principle handles a controlled failure of that uniqueness. Sometimes a convenient construction is not unique. If every outcome is represented the same number of times, we can correct the overcount. Theorem 2.2.1 (Division rule for constant overcounting) Suppose a construction produces representations, and every unique outcome has exactly representations, where . Then the total number of outcomes is . Proof Let us count all the possible representations . We do it in two steps. First we choose the outcome to which it corresponds. Then, having chosen the outcome, we choose the specific representation that gives that outcome. There are outcomes (choices at step 1) There is representations to choose from once the “outcome” has been established ( choices at step 2). The number of choices at each step does not depend on the choices of the other steps so we apply the product rule to obtain: , and therefore , as desired. Warning 2.2.2 (The overcounting factor must be constant) Division is justified only when every outcome is represented exactly the same number of times. If some outcomes have more representations than others, there is no single overcounting factor by which to divide. Next, we will apply this principle to count strings where the order of characters does not matter, but only the characters actually present do."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.3 Unordered selections: combinations without repetition","anchor":"unordered-selections-combinations-without-repetition","text":"2.3 Unordered selections: combinations without repetition Lecture Note 1 introduced Section 1.3 as ordered objects and Section 1.1 as unordered collections. We now use the division rule to pass from ordered representations to unordered selections. First, we introduce an important mathematical notation, the factorial. Definition 2.3.1 (Factorial) For an integer , we define (pronounced “ factorial”) the quantity that is the product of the numbers from to . We also define , matching the convention that an empty product equals . The factorial is closely related to the concept of order. Indeed let us count the ways to order or arrange a total of distinct characters. Example 2.3.2 (Anagrams: n distinct characters) What is the number of anagrams (permutations) of a string of distinct characters? Solution: A permutation of a string of characters is another length- string over the alphabet consisting of those characters, with no repetition. Therefore the number of anagrams (permutations) of a string of distinct characters is Theorem 2.3.3 (Factorial formula for -permutations) The product defining in Theorem 1.3.4 can be written as The division removes all the numbers from downwards from the product in the numerator: as desired. We now have the notation needed to count unordered selections. A -element subset of a set is a subset with . Definition 2.3.4 (Binomial coefficient) For integers and , the binomial coefficient is the number of -element subsets of an -element set. In other words, it is the number of ways to choose different elements from possible options, disregarding the order in which we choose it. Theorem 2.3.5 (Formula for combinations without repetition) For , Proof Let as associate to each set the list of its elements: if with we write Effectively, we associated to a string of characters chosen from the alphabet . We do not allow repetitions since the same element may not be listed multiple times. The number of strings of length from an alphabet of characters is exactly . However, is it true that every string corresponds to a different set? No! if we take a string or any of its premutations, we will describe the same set. Since the number of permutations of a string of distinct characters is we will be overcounting every outcome (set) times. According to the division rule, the number we are looking for is therefore Example 2.3.6 (Choosing a committee) Return to part 1 of Problem 1.2.3: a company has employees and must form a four-person committee. Solution: An outcome is a four-element subset of the set of employees. The employees are distinct, nobody can be selected twice, and positions on the committee are not distinguished. Therefore the number of committees is If we had selected the four employees in sequence, we would have counted every committee times—once for each ordering of its members. Dividing by turns ordered selections into unordered subsets. Warning 2.3.7 (Do not divide merely because the problem says “choose”) Do not divide by merely because a problem uses the word “choose.” First decide what an outcome is and decide whether the order matters We are now ready to solve the second part of the earlier challenge problem. Example 2.3.8 (Choosing a committee with a chair) Return to part 2 of Problem 1.2.3: a company with employees must select a committee with one chair and three other members. Solution 1: Let us split the selection into two steps. First we select the chair. Then, among the remaining 19 employees, we select a subset of 3 to serve as line members of the commitee. Step 1 gives us 20 choices. In step 2 we select a subset of 3 out of the remaining 19 employees. This can be done in ways. Each of these choices yields a valid committee and each possible commitee is the result of a unique choice in our described procedure. Therefore, by the product rule, the total number we are looking for is Solution 2: Let us select the 4 employees to serve on the commitee in order. This can be done in ways. The first person selected is the chair while the remaining 3 are the line members. Each of these orderred selections yields a valid committee, however different selections can lead to the same outcome. For example abcd and acbd both correspond to a commitee with as chair and as commitee line memebers. By how much do we overcount? Any permutation of the last 3 characters gives the same outcome so every outcome is counted times. By the division principle our answer is then"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.4 Counting problems","anchor":"counting-problems","text":"2.4 Counting problems Many combinatorial problems can be solved by modeling them using settings we have become familiar with: ordered strings or unordered subcollections. In particular, if we manage to esablish a “bijection” (a 1-to-1 and onto relationship) between a problem and another one we know how to solve, then the number of outcomes in both has to be the same. Next we provide a list of examples linking common mathematical counting problems to the ones we already studied."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.4.1 Number of subsets of a set","anchor":"number-of-subsets-of-a-set","text":"2.4.1 Number of subsets of a set Example 2.4.1 (Counting all subsets) Let be a finite set with elements. How many subsets does have? In other words: how large is the collection of all sets that are subsets of ? Solution: First, let us number the elements of from to . To a subset , associate the binary string of length as follows: Every subset produces exactly one binary string. Conversely, every binary string specifies exactly one subset by selecting exactly when . Therefore we have established a bijection between the objects we are counting (subsets) and things we know how to count (strings). There are choices for each of the positions, so there are subsets of . For , the formula gives one subset: the empty set itself."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.4.2 Functions from one set to another","anchor":"functions-from-one-set-to-another","text":"2.4.2 Functions from one set to another For sets and , a function assigns exactly one element to each element . Example 2.4.2 (Functions from one set to another) Suppose and , where . How many functions are there? Solution: List the elements of as . A function is determined uniquely by the string For each input , there are possible outputs, with repetition allowed. The product rule therefore gives functions. When , there is one function from the empty set to , corresponding to the empty string, so the value is . Example 2.4.3 (Injective functions from one set to another) Suppose and , where . How many injective functions are there? Solution: We require that different inputs must receive different outputs. As before, list the elements of as . The function is determined uniquely by the string and the function is injective if and only if the string does not have repeating characters. Therefore we have estabished a bijection between injective functions and strings of length of an alphabet of letters. There are such functions. Careful, it is easy to mess up the role of and in the formulas above! One way to help remeber is to try the case when , that is functions from 1 possible input to possible outputs.."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.5 External References","anchor":"external-references","text":"2.5 External References"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.5.1 Additional material (optional)","anchor":"additional-material-optional","text":"2.5.1 Additional material (optional) Discrete Math II - 6.1.2 The Complement Rule and Complex Counting Problems Discrete Math II - 6.1.3 The Subtraction and Division Rules Counting Rocks! - The addition, subtraction, and multiplication principles Counting Rocks! - The Division Principles and Overcounting Counting Rocks! - Combinations"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/index","pageTitle":"Unit I: Enumeration and inclusion-exclusion","heading":"Unit I: Enumeration and inclusion-exclusion","anchor":"unit-i-enumeration-and-inclusion-exclusion","text":"Unit I: Enumeration and inclusion-exclusion Unit I develops precise counting models and the basic rules for combining, subtracting, and correcting counts."},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Access and well-being","anchor":"access-and-well-being","text":"Access and well-being Center for Educational Access Counseling and Psychological Services U of A Cares University emergency information RazALERT"},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Additional references","anchor":"additional-references","text":"Additional references Eric Lehman, F. Thomson Leighton, and Albert R. Meyer, Mathematics for Computer Science, open PDF. Oscar Levin, Discrete Mathematics: An Open Introduction, open edition. Kenneth Bogart, Combinatorics Through Guided Discovery, open edition. Joy Morris, Combinatorics, version 2.1, open textbook. Henry Adams, Kelly Emmrich, Maria Gillespie, Shannon Golden, and Rachel Pries, Counting Rocks! An Introduction to Combinatorics, PDF."},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Main textbook","anchor":"main-textbook","text":"Main textbook M. T. Keller and W. T. Trotter, Applied Combinatorics: web edition and PDF edition."},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Mathematics help","anchor":"mathematics-help","text":"Mathematics help Mathematics Resource and Teaching Center Department tutor request Student Success academic initiatives"},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Resources","anchor":"resources","text":"Resources I will provide lecture notes or detailed references for all topics we cover. I may also include optional videos and other additional material as alternative explanations or extra practice."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Academic integrity","anchor":"academic-integrity","text":"Academic integrity We all share responsibility for creating a course environment built on honest work and learning. Follow the University of Arkansas Academic Integrity Policy. Specific expectations for collaboration and artificial intelligence appear on the assignments page."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Accessibility and accommodations","anchor":"accessibility-and-accommodations","text":"Accessibility and accommodations If you have approved accommodations, request your accommodation letter and contact me early so we can make arrangements in time. If you think you may need accommodations but have not set them up, the Center for Educational Access can help. Temporary conditions can affect access too, so reach out promptly when they arise."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Assessment","anchor":"assessment","text":"Assessment Component Weight Written hand-in homework 20% Online questions 15% Exam 1 16% Exam 2 16% Exam 3 16% Exam 4 16% Course check-in completion 1% Total 100% Grade weights breakdownWritten hand-in homework 20%; Online questions 15%; Exam 1 16%; Exam 2 16%; Exam 3 16%; Exam 4 16%; Course check-in completion 1%.Written hand-in homework: 20%Online questions: 15%Exam 1: 16%Exam 2: 16%Exam 3: 16%Exam 4: 16%Course check-in completion: 1%Written hand-in homework 20%Online questions 15%Exam 1 16%Exam 2 16%Exam 3 16%Exam 4 16%Course check-in completion 1%Course check-ins are brief questions I create to learn what is working for you in the course organization, pacing, and resources."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Attendance and participation","anchor":"attendance-and-participation","text":"Attendance and participation Attendance is not graded, and there is no absence penalty. If you need to miss class, use the posted notes, online questions, homework, and other resources to keep up independently. Please keep track of course material, announcements, deadlines, and assessments. If your work suggests that your current study routine is not serving you, I may reach out with ideas such as regular attendance or office hours. Please plan to be present for scheduled exams. I will not be able to offer make- up exams except for excused emergencies."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Communication, records, and course changes","anchor":"communication-records-and-course-changes","text":"Communication, records, and course changes Email is the best way to reach me with an individual question. I will post course-wide announcements, grades, and updates in Blackboard, so check your University email and Blackboard regularly. If you notice a possible grading or recording error, let me know promptly, within one week of the posted score. I may revise the syllabus when needed. If a change materially affects grading, exams, deadlines, or course delivery, I will announce it in writing. The current version of this website is our course reference."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Course organization","anchor":"course-organization","text":"Course organization In class, I will introduce new topics and talk through their applications with you. After most meetings, online questions provide a quick review of basic skills; they are graded automatically, and most allow multiple attempts. I will assign hand-in homework weekly. We will have three midterms and a final-period exam."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Course overview","anchor":"course-overview","text":"Course overview See the University of Arkansas mathematics course listing. Prerequisites are MATH 26103 or MATH 28003; the pre- or corequisite is MATH 30803 or MATH 30903. You should be comfortable working with sets, functions, relations, basic proofs and mathematical arguments, algebraic manipulation, and polynomials. Combinatorics begins with a simple question: How many ways can we arrange a collection of objects? Can a schedule, pairing, or design be made at all? What changes when two arrangements should count as the same? These questions show up in mathematics, computer science, probability, and more. We will learn foundational combinatorial techniques and the inclusion-exclusion principle, which allow us to count efficiently. We will then learn generating functions as algebraic bookkeeping for combinatorial quantities, followed by recurrence relations that model how later states depend on previous ones. Additional topics may include modular arithmetic and applications to cryptography, coding theory, graph theory, counting arrangements with symmetries, and elementary algebraic structures. Unit Main capability Enumeration and inclusion-exclusion Define objects precisely and count them using cases, bijections, double counting, and overlapping restrictions. Generating-function foundations Encode sequences as formal power series and interpret algebraic operations as counting operations. Recurrence models Select states and initial conditions, derive and solve recurrences, and compare recursive, memoized, and bottom-up evaluation. Further topics To be announced."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Dropped assignments","anchor":"dropped-assignments","text":"Dropped assignments The worst two hand-in homework assignments are dropped. The worst four online-question assignments are dropped. One extra online question is dropped if you complete all check-in work."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Inclement weather and emergencies","anchor":"inclement-weather-and-emergencies","text":"Inclement weather and emergencies We will follow University closure and inclement-weather decisions. If a closure prevents an exam, I will announce a new date through Blackboard and the course website. Please do not travel when conditions are unsafe. University emergency information RazALERT emergency notifications Academic Policy 1858.10: inclement weather"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Learning outcomes","anchor":"learning-outcomes","text":"Learning outcomes By the end of the course, you should be able to: Use basic counting techniques, including permutations, combinations with repetition, and careful accounting for overcounting. Analyze a discrete model by identifying its objects, states, parameters, and constraints. Choose and switch representations to reveal a model’s useful structure. Explain a combinatorial argument clearly: describe the model, choose a representation, and apply the right known techniques. Describe a recursive model and analyze its behavior by identifying state information, a recurrence, and initial conditions, and by finding a closed form when possible. Use generating functions as formal algebraic models and extract the information they encode. Work with selected advanced discrete systems, which may include symmetry, graphs, algorithms, and complexity analysis."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Letter grades","anchor":"letter-grades","text":"Letter grades The following tentative distribution is a reference; I will let you know if it needs to change during the course. Course percentage Letter grade 88–100 A 76–87.99 B 64–75.99 C 51–63.99 D Below 51 F"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Materials","anchor":"materials","text":"Materials Our lecture notes are the main guide through the course. The principal reference is Keller and Trotter, Applied Combinatorics: web edition and PDF edition. Additional references are listed on the resources page; you do not need to purchase any of the listed open materials."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Online questions","anchor":"online-questions","text":"Online questions Online questions normally follow a lecture and are due by the start of the next class meeting. They focus on comfort with key vocabulary and formulas."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Other information","anchor":"other-information","text":"Other information"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Religious observances, University activities, and other excusable conflicts","anchor":"religious-observances-university-activities-and-other-excusable-conflicts","text":"Religious observances, University activities, and other excusable conflicts Email me as early as you can about religious observances, required University activities, military service, jury duty, documented illness, a family crisis, or another excusable conflict. For an exam conflict you can anticipate, letting me know at least one week ahead is especially helpful. We will follow applicable University policies and deadlines. If you have three or more final exams on one day, you may be able to reschedule one under Academic Policy 1500.20. If it involves this course, request it before the Registrar’s withdrawal deadline."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Student support","anchor":"student-support","text":"Student support Mathematics Resource and Teaching Center Student Success Counseling and Psychological Services U of A Cares Additional links are collected on the resources page."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Syllabus","anchor":"syllabus","text":"Syllabus Course Combinatorics MATH 31003-01 Semester Fall 2026 Instructor Gennady Uraltsev Email uraltsev@uark.edu Office SCEN 421 Class meetings Monday, Wednesday, Friday 8:35-9:25 a.m., GEAR 108 Office hours Wednesday 4:00-5:00 p.m., SCEN 421"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Unit exams","anchor":"unit-exams","text":"Unit exams There are four unit exams: three in-class exams and one during the first part of the final-exam period. Every exam problem will be parallel to an assigned homework problem in its required method and main solution steps, though the parameters, objects, or wording may change. Exam Date or period Scope Exam 1 Friday, September 18 To be announced Exam 2 Friday, October 16 To be announced Exam 3 Friday, November 13 To be announced Exam 4 First hour of the Registrar-assigned final exam To be announced Mastery reassessment Second part of the same final period One earlier midterm unit selected by the student During the second part of the final exam period, you may choose to reassess your mastery of one of exam units 1–3. The reassessment covers the same unit and methods. If the reassessment score is higher, it replaces the earlier score; if it is lower, the earlier score remains. For each exam, you may use one standard letter- or A4-size page of handwritten notes that you prepared yourself. If you need an alternate or accommodated testing arrangement, contact me and the appropriate University office at least two weeks in advance."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Weekly hand-in homework","anchor":"weekly-hand-in-homework","text":"Weekly hand-in homework A typical assignment has several problems and may involve computation, modeling, proofs, or other mathematical arguments. Submit it online as a PDF; a handwritten, scanned PDF is welcome. Make sure it is legible, labels each problem, explains the method and final answer, and uses complete mathematical statements. Follow the deadline listed on the assignment page. If you need more time, talk to me in advance of the deadline so we can work out an extension. Unforeseen emergencies are handled case by case."}];
